[06:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'least it resets the whole scene as you can see and we\'re losing the other animation. So, just be aware of that that you don\'t click restart when you want to chain different animations together and rather go then manually back in the timeline or click on restart'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from now rather than the restart itself. So, let\'s do that again. So, this time I\'m choosing here a person is standing first. Then I give it at another point here a person takes a hit and staggers backwards clutching their chest, which I'

[06:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think it reads it very well as you can see here. So, really cool stuff and this way you can then, you know, just combine them together. You can add as many as you want in the given timeline and then just play around with it what feels the'

[07:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'most natural to you and what animations you would like to have. So, the next really crazy thing is that we are now able to control our character'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and where our character should go in the space moving forward. This is of course really nice when you have for example an interior room or something and you would like that it pushes against objects and so on and you can edit this all in real'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time and so on. So, let me show you exactly how to do that now. So, you will see we have here this basic walking animation and what we can do now is that we\'re hitting P in the viewport and you will see that we then enable the'

[07:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'waypoint mode And this way point mode is then able that we can tell our character where it should go in space. So, we can just click on here with left mouse button, and you will see that our character is then running into the'

[07:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'position we would like to have it. Here as well, we can then click on another point in time, and the greatest thing is that NVIDIA already will actually capture where it runs. So, you will see that once we restarted it, it will'

[08:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'remember the motion it had. So, you see at frame 2 100, it knows it needs to go there, and then coming back basically afterwards to the third root point we set. You can also see here that you can, for example, visualize the path by using'

[08:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the use dense root parameter on the right, where it will then also visualize the path for you. But, let\'s say you would like to capture a specific pose, then even if we change the way points'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and some other animations that he still keeps the pose at a specific frame. And this is where constraints coming in. And this is really cool because we can then say, for example, on a specific frame like 125 that we would like to capture'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'his pose now. So, we can then place those points basically in the timeline by just clicking left mouse button, and then you can, for example, key then the left hand, right hand, left foot, and so on. And you will see that NVIDIA is then'

[08:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creating this captured pose, and it will save that then at this point in time for us. Even when you go to restart, it will save that constraint, and it will definitely remember that pose that whatever you else you will do. It will'

[09:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then always go back to this one frame we keyed, and this is of course really useful to then get even more control over your effects. You know, it will then try to calculate the way points first and then going into this one pose. This can be a bit clunky, of course, in'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'case you don\'t animate it properly, but definitely cool stuff to play around with. The third feature I wanted to show you, and this guy has really blew my mind because this is something I haven\'t seen before, is that it actually can'

[09:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capture live human movements, how you control your characters in real time, and then it capture this motion so that you can then use it later in Blender, which I\'m going to show you. What we need to do is first that you hit T in'

[09:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the viewport, and once we hit the T in the viewport, we can then first let him walk again. So, let\'s hit space bar first, and then he starts walking. And now we can then actually press the'

[09:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'arrows on our keyboard, and you will see that he will then follow the direction we give it. So, here he is a bit slow because I first have to press forward, so he gets a bit faster. So, you can also actually control the speed of your'

[10:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character, how fast you want him to walk, and then you can hit with the other arrows, you know, which direction he should take. And this is then all live recorded for you while you control him through the room, for example, or'

[10:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some other walk animations. And you can do that with any walking animations or running animations. All right, and one thing I wanted to do now is to actually combine all three techniques that I just showed you together. So, here I set'

[10:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something like a person swings one arm in a wide horizontal arc while stepping forward, then raises the other arm defensively. And once I generated this, I would then go ahead first and then set the waypoints again with P in the'

[10:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'viewport. Then I\'m going to restart, and you will see that it then follows the motion. And now we can combine it with the target velocity method that I showed you before. So, we can then just replay it [music] and go to restart again. And'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now I can then hit T in the viewport, and now I can then override it. So, basically, I can then say, "Okay, it should still walk to this point in space, but I would like to have a different motion in between." And you'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will see it\'s doing that quite well here. Of course, it can confuse that, you know, when you do it too fast, like I did in this example over here. But anyways, I think it shows the point quite well that that you can then'

[11:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'animate in betweens. And this is then, of course, really useful to then refine your animations. And once we are then happy with our animation, we can then also export it.'

[11:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
